Cell[TextData[{
 "On ",
 StyleBox["Z^d", "TI"],
 " the count is ",
 StyleBox["L_d(r) = \[CapitalSigma]_k 2^k C(d, k) C(r, k)", "TI"],
 ", the sum over ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 " from 0 to ",
 StyleBox["d", "TI"],
 ", with ",
 StyleBox["C", "TI"],
 " the binomial coefficient: a point of the ball has ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 " nonzero coordinates, which take ",
 StyleBox["C(d, k)", "TI"],
 " positions, ",
 StyleBox["2^k", "TI"],
 " signs and ",
 StyleBox["C(r, k)", "TI"],
 " absolute values of sum at most ",
 StyleBox["r", "TI"],
 ". The substrates are finite patches of the lattices. A count follows the \
polynomial while the ball stays inside the patch, and the Riemannian count \
while one more layer does: on the medium cubic grid, to ",
 StyleBox["r = 3", "TI"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["r = 2", "TI"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"9aa14d07-19e3-4a78-b3a5-6c1f9a2cf749"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In the continuum the reference is the small-ball expansion of Gray and \
Vanhecke. On a Riemannian manifold of dimension ",
 StyleBox["n", "TI"],
 ", with ",
 StyleBox["\[Omega]_n", "TI"],
 " the volume of the Euclidean unit ball and ",
 StyleBox["Scal(c)", "TI"],
 " the scalar curvature at the centre, ",
 StyleBox["Vol B_r(c) = \[Omega]_n r^n (1 \[Minus] Scal(c) r\.b2 / (6(n + 2)) \
+ O(r\:2074))", "TI"],
 ". A lattice ball is not a discrete round ball: the scaled path metric of a \
lattice converges to a norm whose unit ball is a square on the square grid \
and a hexagon on the triangular one, and the leading coefficient of ",
 StyleBox["L", "TI"],
 " is the area of that unit ball. The expansion is the reference on a mesh of \
a curved surface."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"0e47e157-99a9-4a02-922b-174aca5edce4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "How the number is measured: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraMeasurement"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraMeasurement", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"InfraBall", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["c", "TI"], ",", " ", 
      StyleBox["r", "TI"]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["measure", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ddb5a5d6-85d2-4884-b922-5ab59252382a"],
 " takes the vertices at distance at most ",
 StyleBox["r", "TI"],
 " from ",
 StyleBox["c", "TI"],
 ", one row of the distance matrix cut at ",
 StyleBox["r", "TI"],
 ", and counts them, or counts its ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraInterior"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraInterior", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "097e56d9-2fc6-497e-bd97-d48ce3d9d522"],
 ". The profile is the list over ",
 StyleBox["r = 0, 1, \[Ellipsis]", "TI"],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["LogDifferenceQuotients"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/LogDifferenceQuotients", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "6108593d-c57a-4cf9-9ff8-b22797cdad14"],
 " reads the dimension off it. On a lattice the quotients of the counting \
profile from ",
 StyleBox["r = 0", "TI"],
 " and of the Riemannian profile from ",
 StyleBox["r = 1", "TI"],
 " coincide: they are the curve of the Wolfram Physics technical \
introduction, section 4.5, which approaches the dimension from above."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"7ec41090-0ae1-4723-a6d4-ea822eaa56d5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A radius past the eccentricity gives the whole graph, and then both \
measures are the number of vertices: the rim of the graph is not a boundary \
of the ball. A band with ",
 StyleBox["r > s", "TI"],
 " gives the empty set."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"ac4f3112-9383-46f8-adc2-c83fd3725348"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The head holds the centre and the radius and computes nothing. A ball has \
one member, the vertex set, so ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["RandomInfraBall"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/RandomInfraBall", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "3c33a91c-99dd-49d9-9129-40881db9c577"],
 " gives it as a sorted vertex list and ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Faithful\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "d8198909-7142-4257-8d41-33e2b36a9cd9"],
 " is ",
 Cell[BoxData["True"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "fb39a21a-a18b-4c68-a122-1e99cb503972"],
 ". ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraMeasurement"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraMeasurement", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "c7a622b2-648d-4d3e-9c4f-9015d41f0edf"],
 " also reads ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<VertexDensity\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "0193492a-4144-4154-b6ee-2d5ac6be50ac"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<EdgeDensity\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "8bbeb65e-4757-451a-91e8-02f3fef17bb8"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Cardinality\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "db1698a0-1d41-48a6-b2f3-029a800a97a8"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Subgraph\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "af401981-6dff-4298-b36a-6a181bf58323"],
 "; a ball has no ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Graph\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4f93b399-02a8-4d0a-ab94-e9fff3c0a428"],
 " and no ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Length\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ada6ae42-6287-402c-8a18-1e783acaba2c"],
 "."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"9caf6588-9df6-417a-aa69-cf406dbd8c5a"],

Cell["Corresponding notions in the classical axiom systems:", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"37b9e8aa-43f2-41f7-b27b-3e25ffe366cc"],

